 "method": "Wall law (Eq. wall, social term; no contact since d > r at the critical width): a lone agent of radius r on the door axis at distance s before the door plane sees the two free jamb ends (10, 5 -+ h), h = w/2, at distance d = sqrt(s^2 + h^2). Lateral parts cancel; the backward force is G(s) = 2 A_w exp((r - d)/B_w) s/d. d ln G/ds = 1/s - s/(B_w d) - s/d^2 = 0 gives s*^2 d* = B_w h^2. The agent is held alone iff G(s*) > F = m v0/tau (Weidmann factor 1 at rest alone). The exact critical width w_c = 2h solves 2 A_w exp((r - d*)/B_w) s*/d* = F. For B_w << h, s* ~ sqrt(B_w h), d* ~ h + B_w/2, s*/d* ~ sqrt(B_w/h), so w_c ~ 2[r + B_w ln(2 A_w/F) - B_w/2 + (B_w/2) ln(B_w/h)]: the effective-diameter form 2[r + B_w ln(2 A_w/F)] plus a geometric correction -B_w[1 + ln(h/B_w)] (about -0.2 m at w = 0.88 m) from the obliquity s/d of the edge force. Population: every spawned agent of the 400 C1 corrected-forward runs at w = 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0 m (seeds 42-141) gets its own w_c; a run is predicted to complete at width w iff no agent has w_c > w (no stuck-alone agent), and the predicted half-completion width is the median over runs of max w_c (bootstrap 95% over runs, 2000 resamples).",
